2. COMPREHENSIVE OVERVIEW OF COMPLETED FUNCTIONS
============================================================

This snapshot contains four active, verifiable components. Everything
else referenced elsewhere in project documentation (automatic parameter
selection, bootstrap uncertainty, compiled fitting engine, predict() and
compare()) is explicitly NOT part of this snapshot — see Section 4.

------------------------------------------------------------
2.1  StatARPLSResult()  —  R/constructors.R
------------------------------------------------------------
Role: the S3 object constructor and structural validator. Every fitting
function in the package returns an object of this class, so this is the
one data shape you need to understand to read anything else.

Inspect its signature and arguments:

    args(StatARPLSResult)

Build a small example directly and inspect the result:

    test_signal     <- c(10, 12, 11, 15, 10)
    test_wavenumber <- 1:5
    test_baseline   <- c(9, 9.5, 10, 10.5, 9)

    result <- StatARPLSResult(
      signal            = test_signal,
      wavenumber        = test_wavenumber,
      baseline          = test_baseline,
      corrected         = test_signal - test_baseline,
      residual_variance = 1.2,
      stability_bounds  = cbind(rep(-1, 5), rep(1, 5)),
      lambda            = 100,
      n_iter            = 5L,
      converged         = TRUE,
      call              = quote(fit_arpls(test_signal))
    )

    class(result)
    str(result)
    print(result)

What to check: class(result) should return "StatARPLSResult", and
str(result) should show all ten fields (signal, wavenumber, baseline,
corrected, residual_variance, stability_bounds, lambda, n_iter,
converged, call) with the types and lengths described in the project's
Design Plan.

------------------------------------------------------------
2.2  fit_arpls()  —  R/fit-arpls.R
------------------------------------------------------------
Role: the core ARPLS baseline-fitting loop, implemented in base R for
this snapshot stage (no compiled code is part of this build yet).

Inspect its signature and arguments:

    args(fit_arpls)

Run it on a synthetic spectrum and inspect the result:

    set.seed(1)
    n <- 200
    demo_wavenumber <- 1:n
    demo_baseline   <- 5 + 0.01 * demo_wavenumber
    demo_peak       <- 50 * dnorm(demo_wavenumber, mean = 100, sd = 5)
    demo_signal     <- demo_baseline + demo_peak + rnorm(n, sd = 0.3)

    fit <- fit_arpls(demo_signal, demo_wavenumber, lambda = 1e5)

    class(fit)
    str(fit)
    fit$converged
    fit$n_iter
    fit$lambda

What to check: fit$converged should be TRUE within the default iteration
limit, and fit$baseline should track the slow linear drift in
demo_baseline without following the sharp peak — plotting it (see 2.4
below) is the clearest way to see this.

------------------------------------------------------------
2.3  print() and summary()  —  R/methods.R
------------------------------------------------------------
Role: console reporting methods dispatched on the StatARPLSResult class.

    print(fit)
    summary(fit)
    class(summary(fit))

What to check: print(fit) should show the call, signal length,
convergence status, iteration count, and selected lambda. summary(fit)
should return an object of class "summary.StatARPLSResult" (check via
the class() call above) with residual quantiles and stability-band
width — not a bare printed list.

------------------------------------------------------------
2.4  plot()  —  R/methods.R
------------------------------------------------------------
Role: base R graphics overlay of the raw signal, the fitted baseline,
and the stability band. No ggplot2 or other plotting package dependency
is used.

    plot(fit)
    plot(fit, show_band = FALSE)

What to check: the first call should draw the raw signal, the fitted
baseline overlaid in a contrasting colour, and a shaded stability band.
The second call should draw the same plot without the shaded band, to
confirm the show_band argument actually toggles it rather than being
decorative.


