3. ADVERSARIAL TESTING AND ERROR HANDLING EXPECTATIONS
============================================================

Per the assignment's requirement to stress-test boundary conditions,
please try the following inputs and compare what you see against what
is documented below. Some of these are handled gracefully by design;
one known case is not yet handled gracefully, and that gap is disclosed
here deliberately rather than left for you to discover.

------------------------------------------------------------
3.1  Mismatched vector lengths — HANDLED GRACEFULLY
------------------------------------------------------------
    fit_arpls(signal = c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5), wavenumber = c(1, 2, 3))

Expected: a clean, custom error message stating that wavenumber must be
the same length as signal, raised by an explicit check inside
fit_arpls() — not a low-level R crash or a cryptic recycling warning.

------------------------------------------------------------
3.2  Malformed stability_bounds matrix — HANDLED GRACEFULLY
------------------------------------------------------------
    StatARPLSResult(
      signal = 1:5, wavenumber = 1:5, baseline = 1:5, corrected = 1:5,
      residual_variance = 1, stability_bounds = matrix(0, nrow = 5, ncol = 3),
      lambda = 100, n_iter = 5L, converged = TRUE, call = quote(f())
    )

Expected: a clean, custom error stating that stability_bounds must have
exactly 2 columns, raised by validate_StatARPLSResult() before the
object is ever returned to the caller.

------------------------------------------------------------
3.3  Non-positive lambda — HANDLED GRACEFULLY
------------------------------------------------------------
    StatARPLSResult(
      signal = 1:5, wavenumber = 1:5, baseline = 1:5, corrected = 1:5,
      residual_variance = 1, stability_bounds = cbind(rep(-1,5), rep(1,5)),
      lambda = -5, n_iter = 1L, converged = TRUE, call = quote(f())
    )

Expected: a clean, custom error stating that lambda must be a single
strictly positive finite number.

------------------------------------------------------------
3.4  Non-scalar converged flag — HANDLED GRACEFULLY
------------------------------------------------------------
    StatARPLSResult(
      signal = 1:5, wavenumber = 1:5, baseline = 1:5, corrected = 1:5,
      residual_variance = 1, stability_bounds = cbind(rep(-1,5), rep(1,5)),
      lambda = 10, n_iter = 1L, converged = c(TRUE, FALSE), call = quote(f())
    )

Expected: a clean, custom error stating that converged must be a single
logical value.

------------------------------------------------------------
3.5  Non-numeric signal input — KNOWN GAP, NOT YET HANDLED GRACEFULLY
------------------------------------------------------------
    fit_arpls(signal = c("a", "b", "c"), wavenumber = 1:3)

Expected at this snapshot stage: this will NOT produce a clean custom
error. Neither fit_arpls() nor the object validator currently checks
is.numeric() on the input vectors — only vector length is checked. A
character vector passed here will fail deep inside the numeric fitting
routine with a generic, less informative base R error (something in the
family of "non-numeric argument to a binary operator" or a matrix
construction failure), rather than failing immediately with a clear
message at the point of input.

This is a genuine, currently-open gap, not an oversight in this
document — please record what error text you actually see when you run
this, since the exact failure point is useful information for closing
it. Explicit is.numeric() input validation is planned before the Draft
Package milestone (Week 10).


