4. ALIGNMENT WITH PROSPECTIVE REFACTORING MILESTONES
============================================================

This section is an architectural update note, not a description of
code present in this snapshot. None of the following is installed by
the devtools::install_github() call in Section 1 — it is documented
here so you understand what is coming and why, and so your review of
the current snapshot is judged against the right scope.

Following supervisor feedback on the package's long-term statistical
design, two changes are being made to the parameter-selection and
uncertainty-quantification code (currently held outside the active
snapshot and staged for the Weeks 10 to 14 development window):

1. Lambda selection is being moved from a simple fixed-grid sweep to a
   fully independent Generalized Cross-Validation (GCV) scoring metric.
   The effective degrees of freedom needed for the GCV score will be
   estimated via a stochastic Hutchinson trace estimator applied to the
   fitted model's hat matrix, computed entirely outside of ARPLS's own
   internal reweighting-loop convergence check — so lambda selection
   depends on an independent statistical criterion, not on whichever
   candidate happened to converge fastest.

2. Baseline uncertainty quantification is being moved from a standard
   independent-and-identically-distributed (i.i.d.) residual bootstrap
   to a Moving Block Bootstrap (MBB). Spectroscopic residuals are
   typically autocorrelated (shared instrument response and unmodelled
   local curvature between neighbouring points), and an i.i.d. bootstrap
   on such residuals understates the true uncertainty of the fitted
   baseline. The Moving Block Bootstrap resamples contiguous blocks of
   residuals rather than individual points, preserving local
   autocorrelation structure in each bootstrap replicate.

Both changes are scoped, documented design decisions in response to
named supervisor feedback, not features you should expect to find or
test in this Week 7 snapshot. If you would like to comment on the
planned design itself (rather than the code you can currently run),
that feedback is welcome and will be recorded separately from your
review of the active snapshot above.


